# Coding Standards

When writing or refactoring code, always follow:

## KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid)
- Prefer the simplest solution that works
- Avoid over-engineering and unnecessary abstraction
- One clear purpose per function/component

## DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Extract repeated logic into shared functions or components
- If you copy-paste more than once, refactor
- Shared types, utils, and UI components belong in common modules

## Modularity
- Split large files into smaller, focused modules
- One component per file (or small related components)
- Components should do one thing well
- Extract reusable pieces: buttons, dialogs, form fields, layouts

## File Structure
- `components/` - UI components (group by feature: `ui/`, `lists/`, `tasks/`)
- `lib/` or `utils/` - shared utilities, helpers
- `types/` - shared TypeScript types
- Keep pages thin: orchestrate, delegate to components

## Anti-patterns to Avoid
- Monolithic page components with 200+ lines
- Duplicated JSX/CSS across files
- Inline styles or class strings repeated everywhere
- Logic mixed with presentation
